% composition-series: Ontario Listening Bridges — set 1
% composition-grade: 2
% composition-elements: pitch (do-re-mi-so-la pentatonic melody), duration (long and short sounds: eighths and quarters in A, half notes, a whole note and a half rest in B), texture (melody over a do-so bordun), form (AB: quick "little bells" A, slow "big gong" B)
% composition-listen: Gamelan Sekar Jaya — "Legong Lasem" (performance video, Gamelan Sekar Jaya official YouTube channel, 30 January 2021; music director I Dewa Putu Berata, dance director Shoko Yamamuro)
% composition-listen-url: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=irmtW9E4Y6Y
% composition-bridge: Listen for nested time: quick interlocking tones inside slower punctuation from larger gongs.
% composition-context: Gamelan names many distinct ensemble traditions of Indonesia, above all in Bali and Java. Gamelan Sekar Jaya is a Balinese gamelan and dance company in Berkeley, California, co-founded in 1979 with the Balinese musician I Wayan Suweca and long taught by visiting Balinese artists. Legong is one of Bali's classical dances, accompanied by a gamelan in which fast interlocking parts move inside a slow cycle marked by gongs. This original pentatonic classroom piece compares quick and slow layers; it is not a gamelan composition and must not be labelled Balinese music.
% composition-distance: Original notes, words and form; no named composition, tuning, kotekan (interlocking) pattern, melody or gong cycle is copied. The classroom pentatonic is the Western do-re-mi-so-la scale, not a Balinese pelog or slendro tuning.
% improv: {"id":"A","bars":"1-4","role":"bell players","prompt":"How quick are the little bells?","choices":["ti-ti on every beat","ta on every beat","beat one only"],"replyBars":4}
% improv: {"id":"B","bars":"6","role":"gong keeper","prompt":"What fills the silence?","choices":["let the gong ring","one soft gong","nothing at all"],"replyBars":1}
% dossier-function: original Grade 2 AB song contrasting short and long sounds: a quick "little bells" A section and a slow "big gong" B section over a do-so bordun, with bell and gong players as layers
% dossier-elements: eight bars in 4/4 with no pickup; pitch set C-D-E-G-A (do-re-mi-so-la), written range C4-A4; A (bars 1-4) moves in eighths and quarters, mostly by step, and ends on so with a half note; B (bars 5-8) uses only half notes, a half rest in bar 6 and a closing whole note on do; B opens with the one leap of a perfect fifth (G4 down to C4) for the gong; form AB
% dossier-practice: Quarter note = 88 is the target; begin at 76. The written range C4-A4 sits inside the young-unison C4-D5 range, and C major pentatonic (no F or B) is the rehearsal key, so barred instruments can be set up with F and B removed. Teach in steps: everyone pats steady quarters; sing A as echoes in two-bar pieces; sing B slowly, counting the long notes aloud (ta-ah, ta-ah-ah-ah); add a do-so bordun (C3 and G3 together) on bass xylophone on beat 1 of each bar; then add bell players on the A section and a gong keeper on the B section, and let the cues change them
% dossier-accompaniment: The classroom accompaniment is a do-so bordun (C and G struck together on beat 1 of each bar) on bass barred instruments, with glockenspiels on the little-bell layer and a low gong, cymbal or drum on the big-gong layer. %%style drone is used only because the Band's drone holds an open do-so fifth, the same sound as the bordun; the page labels that style "tanpura and tabla", which is a South Asian sound, so present the Band as "drone" and never as gamelan or Balinese. No chord symbols are written, so the drone holds C and G throughout
% dossier-lyrics: Original English words written for this record, one syllable per note: "Lit-tle bells, lit-tle bells, ting-a ting-a ting, lit-tle bells, lit-tle bells, hear the gar-den sing. Big gong, ring! Qui-et now." The B words are few on purpose: each long note carries one word, and the half rest in bar 6 is silence for listening. Sung throughout
% dossier-honesty: AI-assisted original composition for Grand Central Stationery, 11 November 2026, under human-directed grade, range and teaching constraints. It is not traditional, not collected from a community and not modelled on "Legong Lasem" or any gamelan piece; the performance is a listening bridge only
% dossier-licence: CC0 1.0. Grand Central Stationery dedicates the melody, words and this ABC encoding to the public domain to the extent legally possible. The "Legong Lasem" video belongs to Gamelan Sekar Jaya, whose description forbids redistribution or commercial use without permission, so the class watches it on the linked channel and nothing from it is included
% dossier-respect: Name Gamelan Sekar Jaya, Berkeley, and the Balinese teachers behind it, and name Bali and legong when playing the video. Explain that gamelan is many traditions, not one style, and that legong grew from sacred dance; treat the gongs and the dance with respect and do not imitate the dancers' costume or movements as a game. Do not call the classroom bells and gong "gamelan", and do not present a US ensemble's video as if it were filmed in Bali
