N:HOW READ (2026-11-11): No source plate exists because this is a new composition. Bar-by-bar audit: A section: bar 1 E-G-A, G-E-D (eighth, eighth, quarter twice); bar 2 C-D-E-D (four eighths), C quarter and a quarter rest; bar 3 E-G-A, A-G-E, the bar 1 motif turned back on itself; bar 4 D-E-G-A (four eighths) and G held as a half note, an open ending on so. B section: bar 5 G half note, C half note (the gong's drop of a fifth); bar 6 E half note and a half rest; bar 7 D and E half notes; bar 8 C whole note, the cadence on do. Every bar totals four quarter beats; the only pitches are C, D, E, G and A (do-re-mi-so-la); range C4-A4; A moves by step and third, and the only leap larger than a third is the perfect fifth G4-C4 at bar 5. Originality: stepwise pentatonic figures are common property; the tune was checked against the GSJ "Legong Lasem" performance, "Hot Cross Buns" (its mi-re-do cadence is avoided; B closes re-mi-do), "Mary Had a Little Lamb", "Frère Jacques" and "Ding Dong Bell" and shares no phrase with them.
N:UNCERTAIN: Whether Grade 2 children hold the B half notes and the whole note for full value, whether the half-rest silence in bar 6 is heard as part of the music, and whether the bell layer at "ti-ti on every beat" crowds the singing are untested. The linked video's description includes history (about the origin of "Legong Lasem") that this record does not repeat until a gamelan educator confirms it; the teacher may prefer a different GSJ video. A classroom trial, a Balinese gamelan educator and a second score reader remain required.
